%I A400185 #73 Sep 24 2026 11:30:50
%S A400185 0,1,2,3,3,5,5,7,7,8,10,10,11,13,13,14,13,17,17,18,17,21,21,22,21,22,
%T A400185 26,26,27,26,27,31,31,32,31,32,31,37,37,38,37,38,37,43,43,44,43,44,43,
%U A400185 45,50,50,51,50,51,50,48,57,57,58,57,58,57,59,55,65
%N A400185 An analog of A399633 for buildings, or, more precisely, poles, on the square spiral on an infinite chessboard.
%C A400185 We start with the square spiral with its cells numbered starting at 0, as in A308884 and A274641. We place poles at the centers of the squares. Note that the squares are indexed starting at 0, in contrast to A399633.
%C A400185 A pole can see another pole if and only if there is a clear line of sight between the tops of the two poles. We assume the poles are line segments, and the tops are single points.
%C A400185 Then a(n) is the smallest number h for which it is true to say that the pole at the center of square n can see exactly h earlier poles. If no such h exists, the sequence ends.
%H A400185 Eric Fox, <a href="/A400185/b400185.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A400185 Jonas Karlsson, <a href="/A400185/a400185.png">Illustration of a(0)-a(49).</a>
%e A400185 The (unfilled) spiral begins:
%e A400185 .
%e A400185 ......................|
%e A400185 .16--15--14--13--12..29.
%e A400185 ..|...............|...|.
%e A400185 .17...4---3---2..11..28.
%e A400185 ..|...|.......|...|...|.
%e A400185 .18...5...0---1..10..27.
%e A400185 ..|...|...........|...|.
%e A400185 .19...6---7---8---9..26.
%e A400185 ..|...................|.
%e A400185 .20--21--22--23--24--25.
%e A400185 .
%e A400185 The completed spiral, showing the heights of the first 25 poles, begins:
%e A400185 .
%e A400185 .13--14--13--13--11
%e A400185 ..|...............|
%e A400185 .17...3---3---2..10
%e A400185 ..|...|.......|...|
%e A400185 .17...5...0---1..10
%e A400185 ..|...|...........|
%e A400185 .18...5---7---7---8
%e A400185 ..|..................
%e A400185 .17--21--21--22--21--
%e A400185 .
%e A400185 After a(0)=0, a(1)=1, a(2)=2, a(3)=3, a pole of height 4 in square 4 would be able to see all four earlier poles. However, a pole of height 3 would see only three earlier poles (its view of the height-2 pole being occluded by the first height-3 pole). Since 3<4, we must take a(4) = 3.
%e A400185 a(9)=8 because a pole of height h = 8 at square 9 can see 8 of the 9 previous poles (all, that is, except the second pole of height 5, at square 6, which is occluded), while no smaller value of h works.
%Y A400185 Cf. A399633.
%Y A400185 See also A308884 and A274641.
%K A400185 nonn,tabf,new
%O A400185 0,3
%A A400185 _Jonas Karlsson_ and _N. J. A. Sloane_, Sep 22 2026
